\begin{remark}[The printed attainment form of the symmetrization lemma]\label{rem:symmetric-strat-limit}
	The source prints Lemma~\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} in the following stronger form (\path{references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex}, lines~94--99): if $\mathfrak G$ is a symmetric game with $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G) = 1-\eps$ for some $\eps \ge 0$, then there exists a symmetric and projective strategy $\mathcal S = (\ket{\psi}, M)$ with $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G, \mathcal S) \ge 1-\eps$. This development does not assert that sentence anywhere. It is recorded verbatim, and unasserted, as the Lean proposition \texttt{PrintedSymmetricProjectiveAttainmentClaim}.

	Two independent defects separate it from the corrected statement. First, the argument of Lemma~\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} yields, for each $\eps' > \eps$, a symmetric projective strategy of value at least $1-\eps'$, and the conclusion at $\eps' = \eps$ does not follow: the dilated dimension $d$ may grow as $\eps' \downarrow \eps$, and the supremum defining $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G)$ in Definition~\ref{def:tensor-product-value} need not be attained by any strategy. Second, on the formal domain the printed sentence is false: Definition~\ref{def:symmetric-game} permits an empty answer alphabet, for which no strategy exists at all while $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G)$ is still defined as the totalized supremum of the empty set, so the hypotheses hold at $\eps = 1$ and the conclusion has no witness. The Lean declaration \texttt{not\_forall\_printed\-Symmetric\-Projective\-Attainment\-Claim} proves exactly this refutation of the universally quantified printed claim.

	There is also a mathematical counterexample with nonempty alphabets. The explicit linear-system game and its symmetric conversion in \cite{gap:qpbt_symmetrization-attainment} give a symmetric game with 419 questions, 16 answers, value one, and no optimal finite-dimensional strategy. Its proof uses Slofstra's independent theorem on a particular linear-system game, together with a normalized Boolean game predicate and a two-copy symmetry conversion. This result has not been proved in Lean; the Lean refutation just named still concerns only empty answers.

	The construction preserves every attainable value, as expressed by
	Lemma~\ref{lem:symmetric-strat-given-strategy}. Together with arbitrary
	positive slack, this justifies the two direct source invocations for their
	value conclusions. The oracularization proof needs only Naimark dilation,
	which preserves Schmidt rank. The answer-reduction proof also claims an
	entanglement bound; its symmetrization doubles Schmidt rank, so preserving
	the state after that step does not by itself prove the printed bound for an
	arbitrary original strategy.

	The compression argument also uses the answer-reduction entanglement
	inequality. It concludes
	\[
		\operatorname{Ent}(V_n^{(2)},1-\eps_2)
		\geq \operatorname{Ent}(V_n^{(1)},1-\eps_1)
	\]
	from answer reduction, where $\operatorname{Ent}(V,p)$ is the minimum
	Schmidt rank needed for success at least $p$ (infinity if unattainable), and
	$V_n^{(1)},V_n^{(2)}$ are the successive verifiers. This rank guarantee remains
	unresolved; the positive-slack value argument does not establish it
	(\path{references/qpbt-paper/13_gap_preserving_compression.tex},
	lines~287--326, especially line~322). The corresponding answer-reduction
	claim is at \path{references/qpbt-paper/11_answer_reduction.tex},
	lines~2110--2115 and 2995--2999. No refutation of those rank claims is asserted.

	Answer reduction and compression are not formalized here.
\end{remark}

